WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.003
- Welcome back for the second session.

00:00:10.751 --> 00:00:13.740
We are delighted to
continue the conversation,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.903
hopefully, inside the room, not outside.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.961
So we are set up for a great 200 session.

00:00:20.961 --> 00:00:24.030
So I'm going to start by
introducing our first speaker

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.300
and then after that we'll
jump directly to my colleague,

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.010
Nick, who will introduce Esther,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.840
and then we'll take questions

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.000
from both of them at the end of it.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.650
So Lucas Chancel

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.290
is an Associate Professor
at Science PO Paris.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.720
He's actually visiting Harvard
Kennedy School this year,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.660
so we were lucky to be
able to get him to come.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.210
His research focuses on global inequality

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:46.950
and environmental policy,

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.530
and he's the co-director
and senior economist

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.300
at the World Inequality Lab

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.620
at the Paris School of Economics.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.853
So, Lucas.

00:00:55.231 --> 00:00:56.700
- Good morning, everybody.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.300
I'm delighted to be here

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.370
and would like to thank the organizers

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.200
of this great conference.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.057
My talk is entitled,

00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:09.270
"Addressing Inequality
Under Climate Change:

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.330
Issues in Measurement and Policy."

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.680
And I would like to start

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.630
with some of the introductory remarks

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.300
and comments made by Rohini earlier today.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.170
I think the introductory
remarks stressed one

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:26.040
of the key achievements
of the past three decades,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.220
which is this reduction of global poverty

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.510
by historic quantities,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.103
by historic numbers, sorry.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:37.893
At the same time,

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:40.260
and is this?

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.093
At,

00:01:41.093 --> 00:01:41.926
sorry.

00:01:41.926 --> 00:01:42.873
At the same time,

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.330
despite this strong
reduction in global poverty,

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.360
which is one of these very positive traits

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.590
of globalization of over
the past three decades,

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.530
economic inequality
remains very large globally

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.130
and the world is still
very far from being flat

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:07.130
as it had been assumed that
it would be in some discourses

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.790
three or four decades ago.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:16.790
There are still huge numbers
of people in extreme poverty

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.130
who might also live side by side

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:24.810
with people with very high
levels of wealth and affluence.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.800
And so what we've also observed
over the past three decades

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.520
is this rise of inequality
within major economies

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.493
in the Global North and
in the Global South.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.060
Climate change tends

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.060
to exacerbate economic inequality
trends between countries,

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.923
and I'll be saying a few words about that.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.840
It also affects inequality
trends within countries

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.680
and it makes more challenging the growth

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.800
of a global middle class.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.230
And so the question that I'm going to ask

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.980
in the coming 15 minutes

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.463
is how to address inequality
on the warming planet.

00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:15.030
And so to kickstart this discussion,

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.480
I'm drawing from a few numbers

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.610
from the World Inequality Report

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:25.050
with my colleagues Thomas
Piketty, Emmanuel Saez,

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.420
and Gabriel Zucman were,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.340
we show here the level of
income inequality on your left

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.760
and the level of wealth
inequality in the world

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.860
on the right hand side of the graph.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.420
So at the global level,

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.420
the top 10% of income earners
make about 50% of all incomes

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.800
and the bottom 50%

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.770
of the world distribution
make a little less than 10%

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.060
of all incomes.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.788
When it comes to wealth and equality,

00:03:53.788 --> 00:03:57.060
either picture is more extreme, so to say.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.200
The top 10% of wealth owners own close

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.940
to three quarters of everything

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.308
there is to own on this planet.

00:04:06.308 --> 00:04:10.470
And the bottom half of the
world distribution owns

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.503
just 2% of global wealth.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.500
So that's the key basic
starting point of the discussion

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.190
that I will try to pursue today.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:27.240
Another important fact I
think is that inequality

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.780
has been rising in many large countries

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.930
or economies over the past
three or four decades.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.030
And here you see the top
10% national income share

00:04:38.176 --> 00:04:40.440
across a few regions or
countries North America,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.340
in blue Europe,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.233
in green Russia, Central Asia,

00:04:44.435 --> 00:04:46.440
in purple China and India in red

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.080
and yellow on this graph.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.690
One of the key insights
from this literature

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:55.770
and economic inequality is
that inequality has been rising

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.623
but it has been rising
at different speeds.

00:04:59.466 --> 00:05:02.310
And so this means that there
are actually different ways

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.360
to open up to international trade.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.560
There are different ways to
embrace new technologies,

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.510
there are different ways to
navigate this changing world

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.086
and to reduce poverty as well.

00:05:18.086 --> 00:05:20.640
And I think this is going
to be one of the argument

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.560
that I will try to make when it comes

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.260
to tackling climate change

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.230
and to dealing with the
inequality issues associated

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.943
with climate change.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:31.633
There are different ways

00:05:31.633 --> 00:05:33.903
in which we can actually
do the energy transition.

00:05:35.357 --> 00:05:37.890
And the question here is,

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:42.480
how is climate change intersecting
with economic inequality

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.390
or perhaps more generally
with inequalities

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.460
in human development?

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.460
And so the discussion on climate change

00:05:52.935 --> 00:05:53.768
and inequality has been,

00:05:53.768 --> 00:05:57.300
and this was also stress
in the introduction

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.990
for a long time, focused on
inequity between generations,

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.300
between now and the future.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.720
For some time now,
scholars have been starting

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.060
to focus more on the now problem,

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.640
inequalities within a generation.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.640
And here I'd like to borrow
some word first point

00:06:17.108 --> 00:06:20.430
from Boyce in his book 2002,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.400
stressing that pollution
is ultimately about winners

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.810
and losers.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.641
Without losers, whether humans

00:06:27.641 --> 00:06:29.820
or non humans,

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.880
alive or yet to be born without losers,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:33.930
there's no pollution,

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.580
but there's no pollution
either without winners,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.130
otherwise either it's a trivial problem

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.940
or it's not going to be sustained

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.160
for a long time.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.620
So it is a distributional matter.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.850
It is a distributional issue
with winners and losers.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.290
Now, when trying to measure it

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:02.280
or to analyze pollution from
an inequality perspective,

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.950
there might be different
ways to think about it.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:08.040
There are different
dimensions, global scale,

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.833
national scale, sub-national
scale, micro level,

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:16.850
different metrics, monetary,
physical, sociodemographic,

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:23.730
and different types of effects
that we might be looking at

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.350
or dimensions here.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:28.770
And equally in terms of
impacts, who is impacted

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.030
by climate change, by environmental change

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.850
or different ways to
contribute to the problem.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:39.630
Who is polluting in what quantities

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.120
through which pollutants,

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.940
but also inequality in capacity

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.000
to face environmental changes

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.020
or inequalities in
capacity to face policies

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.945
hat address interpretable changes.

00:07:54.945 --> 00:07:57.045
And I think the echo has come back, right?

00:07:58.078 --> 00:07:59.980
Very much actually, it is growing

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:03.970
- [An Audience] What
you're saying is so good.

00:08:06.097 --> 00:08:09.453
- Right. So shall we make a
pause or shall I continue?

00:08:10.882 --> 00:08:11.715
Okay.

00:08:13.115 --> 00:08:15.180
So let me just finish this slide

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.910
by saying that this is a burgeoning field

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.473
in the social sciences,

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.590
in which interdisciplinary
research is paramount.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.440
and that's a good news that
I think is an exciting news

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.760
for a diverse audience of scholars,

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.540
of researchers like the one here today.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:41.460
One of the key issues
is that these analyses,

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.940
these metrics on
environmental inequalities,

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.520
on carbon inequalities,
pollution inequalities,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.520
are often missing from
official statistics.

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.520
They're still not quite
present in the dashboards

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.850
used by governments to think
about environmental policy

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.670
or to think about Economic
and Social policy.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:05.760
and now I think that the
echo has actually ended.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.760
So thank you, thank
you very much for that.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.860
And so what I will try to focus

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.552
on in the next few minutes,

00:09:17.552 --> 00:09:20.250
we'll draw from a few recent papers

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.260
that are written down here, plus

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:27.930
some broader work from the
Climate Equality Literature

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:30.540
that will also refer to,

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.490
and the starting point

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:37.290
is what we call here with
my colleague Phillip Bothe,

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.300
and Tancrede Voituriez,

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.810
the Triple Climate Inequality Crisis.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:46.710
So here we try to
represent three dimensions

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:50.190
of global climate inequality,
not necessarily exhaustive,

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:55.190
but we think they map a
fairly good amount of types

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:59.220
of inequalities we might
want to think about.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.630
In red you have the inequality
in relative losses associated

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.340
with climate change.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:06.990
By relative losses here,

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:10.860
I mean that we look at
income losses of individuals,

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:14.550
but we're controlling for
average income differences

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:15.900
between countries.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:18.120
Okay? So these are relative income losses

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.870
after having controlled for
differences in average income

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.010
between countries.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:26.790
And so here we see that the bottom 50%

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:29.080
of emitters at the global level

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.150
in fact make up three quarters

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.550
of all relative income losses associated

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.530
with climate change.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.590
The second dimension in
green is the inequality

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.770
in emissions in pollution.

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:47.640
Some have called this type
of inequality and emission

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.490
and inequality in gains, there are losers

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:51.750
and there are winners.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:54.810
And so pollution can also be seen

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:57.150
or contributions to CO2 emissions

00:10:57.150 --> 00:11:02.150
can also be seen as mapping
fairly well the gains associated

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:06.240
with climate change and
with this pollution.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.300
And so here the other inequality

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:13.110
that is represented here is this one

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.740
by which the top 10% of
global emitters represent more

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:21.540
or less a little less
than 50% of all emissions.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:26.540
And then the third category
here is the inequality

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:30.150
and capacity to finance the transition

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:33.060
or the inequality to face

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:36.150
in ability to face climate policies

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.820
to be resilient in the face of it.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.160
And here this,

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.850
this is basically relatively
badly proxied so to say,

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.450
and I think there's much more
work to be done on the matter

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.270
y basically the Inequality
and Wealth ownership

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:52.590
at the global level.

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:55.410
So basically restating what I was showing

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.350
before, the top 10% of wealth owners make

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:02.940
around three quarters of global wealth.

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:06.610
So that is the, that is
the general framework

00:12:07.543 --> 00:12:09.480
of this intervention.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:12.630
Today I will be looking
at these three types

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.030
of climate inequalities

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:18.665
and for each type I will review

00:12:18.665 --> 00:12:23.580
ome key findings from recent research

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:26.370
and discuss some issues associated

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.448
with the current state of thinking.

00:12:29.448 --> 00:12:34.270
So an unequal losses GDP

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:36.540
impacts associated

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.910
with temperature increases have been found

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:43.890
to be quite unequally
spread across the world

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:46.560
with climate change tending
to exacerbate inequality

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.030
between countries.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:52.050
And so tropical and
subtropical nations appear

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:55.290
to be more impacted by climate
change, by the climate change

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.300
that has already been happening.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.610
And this is largely through the channel

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:03.510
of agricultural productivity impacts,

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:06.513
which we've discussed earlier today.

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:11.850
Now it is also quite,

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:16.230
and so we may wanna discuss
how, sorry, how these,

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.960
these estimates are produced.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:21.660
What are the underlying models used

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:24.243
to produce such estimates?

00:13:25.258 --> 00:13:26.091
owever,

00:13:26.091 --> 00:13:30.780
this inequality of impacts
I think is is something

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:35.070
that is quite well established.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:36.240
As well as the fact

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.030
that within countries,
not just between countries

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:42.550
but within countries, poorest
groups tend to be more exposed

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:47.700
to environmental changes,
to environmental damage.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:52.700
And when environmental
damage hits populations,

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:56.460
poorest groups are also
more vulnerable to it.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:14:01.050
And so this leads to
climate change leading

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:05.486
o higher inequalities
within countries as well.

00:14:05.486 --> 00:14:06.600
And here Hallegatte

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.010
and Rosenberg for instance,

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:12.970
have this interesting result
looking at household surveys

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:15.030
for dozens of countries

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:20.030
and simulating shocks associated
with environmental damages,

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:24.060
they find that the bottom
50 40% of the distribution

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:28.950
loses 70% more than the average
due to environmental shock.

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:31.190
So the bottom 40%, the poorest groups

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:35.190
of the population more
impacted by the average.

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:36.240
y the way,

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.570
that kind of study here looks
at developing countries.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.420
But the literature has also found

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:47.070
that this is not just a
developing country characteristic.

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:50.130
We also see strong climate inequalities

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.830
and impacts in rich countries as well.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:56.700
So it has been shown for instance
that heat waves are going

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:01.700
to hit socioeconomically
most deprived group

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.440
more than the average for instance,

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:07.320
and here I'm just referring to this work

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:12.320
y Obradovich and Co-authors
were they look at differences

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.390
between low income,
high income women, men,

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:19.770
individuals when it comes to
the effect of heat exposure

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:24.000
on the probability of
reporting mental health issues.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.970
And so this is also maybe an area

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:31.970
where more work can be
done to make such results

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:34.290
even more granular and more precise.

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:37.050
But there's a strong
case here of intersection

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:41.643
between gender class and
climate inequalities.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:47.463
Now some issues in this
inequality of losses stream

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:53.850
f the literature I think
is that standard approaches

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.280
to measuring climate
risk or climate impact

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:57.510
can lead to mist targeting.

00:15:57.510 --> 00:16:01.950
So here what this global map
shows is the economic value

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:04.590
at risk of flooding
across the world measured

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:08.340
in US dollar purchasing power parity.

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:11.790
And so you see some form of distribution

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:13.803
of these losses across the globe.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:19.170
Now when the question you're
gonna ask might be a question

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:23.120
of what's the number of people
in poverty that are affected,

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:26.370
he map might look pretty
radically different here.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:31.350
So here this is the population
share which is below a dollar

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:35.220
1.9 poverty threshold and
which is also at flood risk.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.740
So we have a very different representation

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:39.900
of what this inequality is about.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:43.890
So I think that what I've
been trying to discuss

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:47.190
here is that there's this
need for very granular data.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:49.786
We've been looking at that earlier today

00:16:49.786 --> 00:16:53.250
and I think there is a strong
evolution in recent work here.

00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:58.096
Also mobilizing large
scale satellite imagery

00:16:58.096 --> 00:17:01.890
o look at these issues.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:04.770
But there's also a need
for the use of combination

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:07.380
of monetary and non-monetary metrics

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:09.210
to understand these inequalities.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.070
And so this is again this strong call

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:15.960
for even more interdisciplinary
research on the matter.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:18.570
The challenges ahead, I
think, again connecting

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:20.370
with some of the discussion earlier today

00:17:20.370 --> 00:17:22.830
is how to produce real-time estimates,

00:17:22.830 --> 00:17:26.646
how to produce forecasts
that can be used by policy

00:17:26.646 --> 00:17:29.610
o target their social policy
measures, for instance,

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:32.760
to anticipate climate
shocks and their impacts

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.470
and also how to think about

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.980
and how to measure the joint
distribution of consumption

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:39.630
of income and capital

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.810
and how these are impacted
by climate change.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:45.840
And this is something which
I think still quite a bit

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:47.520
of work can be done.

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:50.340
It's already quite difficult
to measure the consumption

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:54.750
of income capital and consumption jointly.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:57.840
nd it's even more complex to simulate

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.700
or to estimate

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.723
how climate change might
affect these distributions.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:09.630
Now let me look at the
inequality of contributions.

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:12.810
So the inequality of contributions.

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:15.273
So that was my green bars here.

00:18:16.401 --> 00:18:19.656
The first way to look at
the problem is really the,

00:18:19.656 --> 00:18:22.170
this historical inequality
of contributions here.

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:27.150
So over 1850 to 2020 North America

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:32.150
and Europe represented
about half of all emissions.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:37.710
and so the key question is how
to split the remaining budget

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:40.110
across countries and across individuals.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:42.630
But this first big dimension

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:45.513
of inequality of contributions.

00:18:46.718 --> 00:18:48.270
Another way to look at this is to look

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:52.980
at the inequality in average
contributions across the world.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:57.853
So global average GHG emissions per capita

00:18:57.853 --> 00:19:02.853
around six tons per person in 2019

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:08.730
with Sub-Saharan Africa around
1.6 tons per person per year

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.800
on average in North America,

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:14.043
a little more than 20 tons per
person per year on average.

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:18.660
Now, sorry, one of the big question

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.960
is then how are we gonna
split these averages

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.530
across the distribution.

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:26.610
So not looking just as at averages,

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:29.370
but looking at the inequality
between individuals,

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:31.950
there are many different ways to do it.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:36.300
The bottom line I'd like
to to say is in this graph

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:39.011
these are results for
bottom 50%, middle 40%,

00:19:39.011 --> 00:19:41.460
top 10, top 1%.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.470
so these are the share in total emissions,

00:19:43.470 --> 00:19:44.970
the results from different studies.

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:46.140
The bottom line

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:48.660
is that these various studies
mobilizing different types

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.850
of methodologies seem to come

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:53.103
up with relatively similar numbers.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.590
And what are these numbers?

00:19:55.590 --> 00:20:00.590
The top 10% of the
distribution of income earners

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:03.390
or consumers or emitters represent more

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:06.580
or less 45% of all emissions

00:20:08.124 --> 00:20:10.770
and the bottom half of
the world population emits

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:14.563
between seven, 7% and 15% of the total.

00:20:14.563 --> 00:20:19.563
So that's a relatively standard
result in this literature.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:22.323
Another result,

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:25.980
this one from one specific study

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:28.020
and with all due respect

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.030
for our Prime Minister Indira Gandhi

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:32.613
that was referred to earlier,

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:37.290
in fact what we see here is that poverty

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:40.590
in itself is not the big chunk

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:41.940
of the problem at the global level

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.520
when it comes to CO2 emissions here.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:47.696
Bottom 50% of the world
population, less than 10%

00:20:47.696 --> 00:20:49.440
of emissions, but even the eradication

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:53.580
of extreme poverty itself
would not represent according

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:55.020
to Bruckner and Co-author,

00:20:55.020 --> 00:21:00.020
such a big increase in overall emissions.

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:04.500
So this is top 1% emissions
compared with emissions required

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:06.810
to eradicate $1.9

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:10.140
or 3.2 a dollar poverty.

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:14.400
So the big question is the
growth of the middle class.

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:18.780
It is not the eradication
of extreme poverty.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.240
Now another result from this literature

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:26.010
I think is there has been
somewhat of a big shift

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.070
in the relative importance of

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:33.150
between versus within
country carbon inequality

00:21:33.150 --> 00:21:35.100
over the past four decades.

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:37.140
So this is a Theil decomposition

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:41.820
of the global inequality in
individual carbon emissions.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:46.820
And so according to these
results in the early 1990s,

00:21:48.832 --> 00:21:51.720
a little more than 60%
of global inequality

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:54.360
is due to between country differences.

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:57.000
So to average differences between China

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.870
and US Europe for instance,

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:04.860
this inequality has
been reducing over time.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:09.860
And now we are in a world
where most of the inequality,

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:12.240
most of global carbon equality

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:15.035
is due to within country differences.

00:22:15.035 --> 00:22:17.580
This doesn't mean that
there aren't big differences

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:21.040
in average emission levels between regions

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:25.358
and I refer to one of the
earlier graphs I've presented.

00:22:25.358 --> 00:22:27.864
But it means that on top of
this we're now in a world

00:22:27.864 --> 00:22:29.550
where there's also quite a big amount

00:22:29.550 --> 00:22:31.923
of inequalities within countries.

00:22:34.647 --> 00:22:39.579
Let me in the interest
of time, so two minutes,

00:22:39.579 --> 00:22:42.780
three minutes, alright,
in the interest time,

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:46.000
just maybe flagging some key issues

00:22:47.524 --> 00:22:51.270
in this literature on the
attribution of emissions

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:54.510
to individuals, it is
quite straightforward

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:58.560
to attribute direct
emissions to individuals

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:00.293
or to households.

00:23:00.293 --> 00:23:02.760
So think about the carbon
that you emit when you use

00:23:03.603 --> 00:23:05.760
your car that uses gasoline

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:07.410
or think about the emissions

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:10.228
from your gas heater for instance.

00:23:10.228 --> 00:23:13.324
That's really easy to
attribute to individuals.

00:23:13.324 --> 00:23:15.469
When it comes to attributing all emissions

00:23:15.469 --> 00:23:17.850
within that are associated

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:20.760
with production processes to individuals.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.203
It's more complicated.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:28.220
One of the IPCC gives
basically two criterions

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:32.866
to do so one is that of exclusivity.

00:23:32.866 --> 00:23:34.680
You don't wanna count the
same ton of carbon twice

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:36.390
for two different actors,

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:39.513
but also of you need to be comprehensive.

00:23:39.513 --> 00:23:41.940
So you wanna count direct
and also in direct emissions.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:45.600
And to do this in a systematic
framework at the level

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:47.325
of a country as a whole

00:23:47.325 --> 00:23:48.720
and even more at the level of the world

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:51.177
is quite challenging.

00:23:51.177 --> 00:23:52.539
And so the standard approach

00:23:52.539 --> 00:23:54.000
to that has been to
distribute all emissions

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:57.600
to consumers using input output tables.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:02.190
So trade tables connected
with pollution data

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:05.133
and to allocate all these
emissions to consumers.

00:24:06.011 --> 00:24:10.713
And I think that's a good way to proceed.

00:24:10.713 --> 00:24:14.460
At the same time, this
tends to miss the role

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:19.460
of savings, the role of
investors in driving emissions

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.730
or in driving decarbonization.

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:28.410
This tends to omit the
fact that as consumers

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.600
we sometimes have limited information,

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:32.641
limited agency.

00:24:32.641 --> 00:24:34.470
And so this approach

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:38.241
to measuring emissions
solely focused on consumer

00:24:38.241 --> 00:24:42.619
is actually in some ways limited.

00:24:42.619 --> 00:24:46.710
And just to give you an
insight of what I'm trying

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:49.740
to say here, this is the distribution

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:54.360
of carbon footprint in the US in 2019.

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:57.090
That's a consumer perspective.

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:58.670
Full population,

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:00.270
a little more than 20
tons per person per year.

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:03.000
The top 10% of the
population here is a little

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.940
above 50 tons per person per year.

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:10.200
Now if I'm doing the same thing,
so attributing US emissions

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.033
to US individuals,

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:15.750
if I'm attributing direct emissions,

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:17.400
again to those who are responsible

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.278
for these direct emissions.

00:25:19.278 --> 00:25:22.050
But if I am attributing emissions
from production processes

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:26.490
in a different way, not
to the final consumers

00:25:26.490 --> 00:25:27.510
of goods,

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:32.400
but to the owners of the
industries that pollute,

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:37.083
this is the kind of picture
that I would have the top 10%

00:25:37.083 --> 00:25:42.083
in that case emits a hundred tons

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:44.630
of CO2 per person per year.

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:45.463
So a factor two different.

00:25:45.463 --> 00:25:47.130
So this is,

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:51.510
and I'll skip all this in the
interest of time just to say

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:54.780
that the consumption
approach has been very useful

00:25:54.780 --> 00:25:58.350
to think about emissions
responsibility and inequality,

00:25:58.350 --> 00:26:00.250
but it might be quite limited

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:02.550
and better measuring emissions associated

00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:03.930
with wealth is key.

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:07.590
This might lead us to
having other new insights

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:10.500
of how to think about the
incidents of carbon taxes

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:12.436
and of regulations.

00:26:12.436 --> 00:26:14.310
And I will skip the final part

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:18.750
which was really about
this third dimension here.

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:20.100
The capacity to finance.

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:23.460
Maybe just by saying that we
might think from this graph

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:26.311
that this is essentially a Global North

00:26:26.311 --> 00:26:29.100
versus a Global South
issue, capital in the North

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:30.963
and relative losses are in the South.

00:26:32.573 --> 00:26:35.640
However, there are also very
large wealth inequalities

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:39.160
in each region of the world.

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:40.877
So even in Europe,

00:26:40.877 --> 00:26:43.560
which is a relatively
equal region in this world,

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:46.020
the bottom 50% of the population owns

00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:50.310
less than 5% of wealth.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:52.590
So this has implications
in how we're gonna think

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:56.518
about bridging the global
climate finance gap,

00:26:56.518 --> 00:26:58.538
who should bridge this gap?

00:26:58.538 --> 00:27:00.810
Private actors, public actors,

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:03.693
how to think about that
in a context of strong,

00:27:04.559 --> 00:27:06.360
relatively strong inequalities.

00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:07.530
And how to think about that in a context

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:11.790
where the investments we
make today are not only going

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:14.070
to determine the ecological state

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:16.630
of the world in the
future, but also contribute

00:27:17.526 --> 00:27:21.630
to shape economic inequalities
in the decades to come.

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:24.284
Thank you very much for your attention.

00:27:24.284 --> 00:27:27.284
(audience clapping)

00:27:33.835 --> 00:27:35.610
- So wonderful.

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:38.707
Thank you for this introduction

00:27:38.707 --> 00:27:42.154
and then sort of tour of the
facts on climate inequality

00:27:42.154 --> 00:27:44.733
in particular this focus
on bridging the gap.

00:27:44.733 --> 00:27:47.850
So we're now gonna turn the
attention in the conference

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.260
towards exactly that.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:51.120
What can governments and markets

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:53.130
do to address some of the problems

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:56.610
that we've seen in these
first two sessions?

00:27:56.610 --> 00:27:58.578
And to kick that off,

00:27:58.578 --> 00:28:01.440
I'm very pleased to introduce
Professor Esther Duflo.

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:05.624
Professor Duflo is the
Abdul Latif Jameel Professor

00:28:05.624 --> 00:28:07.140
of Development Economics
and Poverty Alleviation

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:09.810
in the Department of Economics at MIT.

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:12.750
She's the co-founder and co-director

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:15.870
of the Abdul Latif Jameel
Poverty Action Lab,

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:19.170
which really sort of led the innovation

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:20.890
of using randomized experiments

00:28:21.791 --> 00:28:24.661
as we saw in the first session
in development economics.

00:28:24.661 --> 00:28:27.300
And her own research has
spanned an incredible range

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:29.230
of topics within development

00:28:30.303 --> 00:28:33.603
from credit and finance
to education and health,

00:28:34.622 --> 00:28:39.520
governance, inequality,
and environment as well,

00:28:40.704 --> 00:28:43.501
as well as contributions
to empirical methods.

00:28:43.501 --> 00:28:47.666
So this is really a remarkable set of work

00:28:47.666 --> 00:28:49.440
and it's been recognized broadly including

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:53.010
with the inaugural 2008 BBVA
Frontiers of Knowledge Award

00:28:53.010 --> 00:28:56.010
and the 2019 Nobel Prize in economics.

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:58.143
So it's a great pleasure.

00:28:58.143 --> 00:28:59.663
Please welcome Professor Duflo.

00:29:00.689 --> 00:29:03.689
(audience clapping)

00:29:06.690 --> 00:29:08.493
- Thank you. Thank you so much.

00:29:09.639 --> 00:29:12.840
And by way of preliminary,
like an interloper,

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:15.840
I have done some work
on environment with Nick

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:20.343
with among others, but
it's been some time,

00:29:23.949 --> 00:29:25.320
and what I'm going to
talk about today involves

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:27.360
almost no randomized control trial,

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.820
although it has some plug for them.

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:32.980
So I kind of one of these older people

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:39.680
who use their previous work to
justify shouting their mouth

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:42.000
about everything.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:42.833
So I apologize for that,

00:29:42.833 --> 00:29:46.890
but a lot of what I'm going

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:49.353
to say are things that you guys know,

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:54.600
but maybe that's also giving kind of a way

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:57.531
of packaging it for the rest of the world,

00:29:57.531 --> 00:29:58.364
which might also be helpful.

00:29:58.364 --> 00:29:59.790
And some of you are present
the rest of the world.

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:01.743
So you can be my test audience.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:05.873
So,

00:30:05.873 --> 00:30:08.073
oops, that's not the yes,
that's the one I want.

00:30:09.074 --> 00:30:10.373
I want to start with John Kerry.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:16.530
So this is kind of what we
have to, when we, you know,

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:18.720
motivated by Lucas took

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:21.660
or took earlier this morning,

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:24.390
we might think that, you
know, one of the key question

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:27.543
we have to address is
what is our response?

00:30:27.543 --> 00:30:30.600
What is the responsibility
of the rich world reserve,

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:31.710
the poor part of the world

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:35.130
or rich citizen with a poor citizen?

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:37.620
And it doesn't start very well

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:41.310
because the climate unrave
the US is that, you know,

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:45.120
sets the stage in June by
testifying in front of Congress

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:47.460
that the US under no circumstances

00:30:47.460 --> 00:30:49.680
will pay climate reparations.

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:52.586
So you would think that's not a very good,

00:30:52.586 --> 00:30:54.750
you know, it's kind of a
pretty clear way to start

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:59.750
the negotiations for Kind
of the sharing of the boost,

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:05.340
the mitigation of climate
change moving forward

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:08.460
and the cost of the adaptation

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:09.810
that has already happening.

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:13.890
But I think it is very
important to just take

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:16.900
that as a given 'cause I
think it is true the US

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:19.050
will under no circumstance
pay reparations.

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:21.750
So I think it's just as a positive matter

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:23.580
at a normative level,

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:26.670
we of course you might disagree

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:30.090
at a positive level, I
think it's helpful to take

00:31:30.090 --> 00:31:33.588
that under advisement and
say this is not happening.

00:31:33.588 --> 00:31:36.960
But of course it's not just
about past responsibility.

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:39.240
So it's not just about reparations

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:41.970
because as Lucas told us,
the the mission responsible

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:46.020
for climate change are mainly
due to the current behavior

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:47.670
of rich citizens, most of which,

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:50.073
although not all of which
are in rich countries.

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.590
I plan to spend very little time

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:57.480
on this because I knew
that Luca was before me,

00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:00.840
but I think one point that
was not, he didn't highlight

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:03.300
as much as it does in in most of his work

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.460
and which is really
essential to remind people,

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:09.930
although I do know it in this,
you know, the second line

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:11.940
after the US will pay reparation

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:14.190
and on no circumstances usually, well yes,

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:17.670
historically it was us, but
now look at China, they are in,

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:19.650
they are the ones, they are the problem.

00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:24.210
So unless China fixes
their emission problem,

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:26.639
then you know, there is nothing we,

00:32:26.639 --> 00:32:29.130
you know, it's going to be
limited what can be done.

00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:30.990
You hear a lot of that in Europe,

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:33.570
which is kind of self-righteous feeling

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:37.230
that we're already kind
of ahead in the combat.

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:39.690
So yes, you know, but you know, had China

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:41.070
and then the US of course

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:44.430
and then India, they are
the one who are emitting

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:46.140
already a lot and more and more.

00:32:46.140 --> 00:32:48.660
So frankly this is to them,

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:53.613
towards them that you have
to focus your attention.

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:56.520
But of course China

00:32:56.520 --> 00:32:59.370
and India are producing a lot of goods

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:03.160
that are then consumed
in the rest of the world

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:06.660
and China and India are net CO2 exporters

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:09.766
and USA is a very big importer.

00:33:09.766 --> 00:33:11.550
So is Europe.

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:12.480
So in other word,

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:15.924
when we thinking about using
this input output table

00:33:15.924 --> 00:33:18.960
to think about the full carbon
footprint of a person we need

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:22.177
to take into account when
they drive the electric car,

00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:26.820
they are the, the full
carbon footprint of someone

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:30.933
is whatever went to
produce said electric car.

00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:33.810
You all know that this is so obvious

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:37.020
that Lucas didn't remind us
that this is how we are trying

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:39.960
to, that it needs to be computed.

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:43.950
But that's not the headline numbers

00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:48.030
when we think about emission
that come to the news.

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:50.310
It's not the consumption emission,

00:33:50.310 --> 00:33:52.470
let alone the wealth emission.

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:53.853
It's just the emission.

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:57.300
So I think the first thing
is to remind that it's a lot

00:33:57.300 --> 00:34:00.030
of work obviously to do properly,

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:05.030
but as Luca emphasized, many
people have done it several,

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:06.660
not many, but several groups have done it.

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:10.440
And in particular he cited
Bruckner and himself.

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:12.330
The methodology are really different

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:15.210
to arrive at very similar results.

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:17.580
Both of them are a lot of
assumptions you might quibble

00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:18.660
with all of the assumptions,

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:20.458
but the fact that they lead

00:34:20.458 --> 00:34:22.163
to very similar result is encouraging.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:26.280
Lucas was a bit more subtle
in describing his own results

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:27.390
than I will be,

00:34:27.390 --> 00:34:30.750
but I like to remind
them by this 10/50 rule,

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.720
10% of the highest polluter
worldwide are responsible

00:34:33.720 --> 00:34:38.340
for almost 50, for about 50%,
almost 50% of global emission.

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:42.000
And conversely, 50% of the
lowest polluters are responsible

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:43.800
for about 10% of global emission.

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:44.970
It didn't have to be that way.

00:34:44.970 --> 00:34:47.610
In fact it's not exactly that way,

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:49.503
but it's easy to remember.

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:55.940
Since we have a Bruckner, the
Bruckner is the world bank.

00:34:56.130 --> 00:35:00.300
The team found very similar results

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:01.800
and they have a nice map.

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:03.903
So I thought I would switch to them.

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:08.732
And then when you look at
where these people are located,

00:35:08.732 --> 00:35:10.980
it is true that there
is rich people in who,

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:15.120
who emit a lot in India and
China in the Middle East also.

00:35:15.120 --> 00:35:17.310
But it tends to be the case

00:35:17.310 --> 00:35:20.673
that high polluters mainly
live in rich countries.

00:35:22.596 --> 00:35:24.720
So this is the map of the carbon footprint

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:26.853
and of course the Sahel region of Africa

00:35:26.853 --> 00:35:31.853
that we heard about this
morning is entirely blue

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:34.410
while the US is is bright red.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:38.070
And with that notion of emission,

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:41.010
Europe is actually kind of orangeish

00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:42.600
whether it would look better

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:45.543
if we didn't include consumption,

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:47.880
if we didn't include kind

00:35:47.880 --> 00:35:50.433
of imputed emission from what we consume.

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:54.390
This is something Lucas has already said,

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:55.863
which is quite important.

00:35:57.600 --> 00:35:59.760
I'll show you a little bit later that,

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:01.680
but again it was mentioned this morning,

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:04.620
and you will know that
there is a lot of kind

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:08.640
of emphasis on mitigation
effort in poor countries

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:10.830
and this idea that there would be a trade

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:15.403
off of between reducing
poverty and fighting

00:36:18.330 --> 00:36:22.410
and avoiding making climate
change worse, but first order

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:26.700
because people in Africa
emit almost nothing,

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:28.740
even if their income were to double,

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:30.780
that would really not
make a big difference.

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:34.140
So this kind of a non-discussion to have

00:36:34.140 --> 00:36:36.750
and for a non-discussion, it has a lot

00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:38.823
of place in the public discussion.

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:43.710
So one summary of this first discussion.

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:45.870
So Lucas at even a smaller number,

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:47.250
but, so the way I read

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:49.920
this is that eradicating extreme poverty,

00:36:49.920 --> 00:36:52.950
so making nobody lives under $2 a day,

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:55.420
would increase emissions by only 2%.

00:36:56.495 --> 00:36:59.790
This probably represents,
you know, a tiny amount,

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:03.630
a tiny fraction of what richest
people in the world consume.

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:07.290
So we can kind of forget
that trade off in the sense

00:37:07.290 --> 00:37:11.553
that first order, first of all,

00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:16.920
poor people are extremely
poor and need to be less poor.

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:19.800
Now second of all, even if
we are only we're worrying

00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:23.850
about climate, of course being poor

00:37:23.850 --> 00:37:28.850
is a pretty bad deal to be
protected against climate.

00:37:29.940 --> 00:37:34.610
The first order way of
adapting to climate change

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:36.660
is to have a bit more
money so that you can,

00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:38.490
you don't have to work when it's very hot,

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:41.840
you can adapt your production
and so on and so forth.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:44.493
So that is kind of a first
point I wanted to make.

00:37:45.450 --> 00:37:48.810
The second point is again,
as Lucas already said,

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:50.190
the cost of climate change are going

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:52.443
to be felt in the poor part of the world.

00:37:53.584 --> 00:37:54.570
He emphasized income

00:37:54.570 --> 00:37:59.072
and I want to emphasize
mortality from the work by,

00:37:59.072 --> 00:38:02.040
you know, people in this
home global impact lab.

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:04.020
But first, the first order point,

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:07.473
of course poor country are in
places that are already hot.

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:10.503
So this is temperature today.

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:15.030
And therefore, even though,

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:18.789
you know, suppose the earth
warmth by two degrees,

00:38:18.789 --> 00:38:20.790
it's not going to be two
degrees absolutely everywhere.

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:23.163
But just to simplify,
imagine that's the case.

00:38:24.476 --> 00:38:25.680
If you are in a place where
there are already many

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:26.670
very hot days,

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:29.070
then you're going to get
more of those hot days.

00:38:30.335 --> 00:38:33.720
And then of course the
impact of temperature

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:38.010
on the human body's ability to thrive

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:42.840
and then to survive is not
linear in particular, you know,

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:45.060
above 30, 32 in particular,

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:47.340
if it's a little bit human on top of that,

00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:51.870
is where it becomes just not
very congenial for human life.

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:55.530
And you are going to see
more of those hot days

00:38:55.530 --> 00:38:56.823
in poor countries.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:38:59.930
Already we see them more today.

00:39:00.785 --> 00:39:03.360
I realized that I'm
keeping on your advisement

00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:04.443
from this morning using the fact

00:39:04.443 --> 00:39:06.840
that it's happening today already,

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:08.340
but you know, this is 2050,

00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:11.043
soon enough we are going
to get more the hot days.

00:39:12.219 --> 00:39:14.970
I should note this, the
North of Latin America

00:39:14.970 --> 00:39:19.500
is also to be kind of gets
very red in these places.

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:23.760
And by, I think I skipped
one slide, by 2000,

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:24.960
it's even more hot days.

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:27.990
And you see a huge
concentration of the number

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:32.370
of very hot days in places

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:36.570
that are very poor in
particular Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:38.670
but also Pakistan

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:41.553
and the Northeast of Brazil for example.

00:39:42.690 --> 00:39:45.433
The second point also from Tara Carlton

00:39:47.167 --> 00:39:50.000
and others, this a big great group

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:53.820
of people at Chicago looking at

00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:56.040
and kind of a lot of people they work

00:39:56.040 --> 00:40:01.040
with all over have used
historical data to try

00:40:03.540 --> 00:40:07.530
and compute the cost
again in term of human,

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:11.430
of human mortality of an extra hot days.

00:40:11.430 --> 00:40:14.936
So what you can do is to say,
well it's been, you know,

00:40:14.936 --> 00:40:18.090
in the past around the climate

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:20.400
there is variation from year to year.

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:22.560
Take a particular district in India

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:26.430
where there are more hot days,
do more people die compared

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:27.960
to a very similar district

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:31.860
where in this particular years
there are fewer hot days.

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:34.170
So that's, we can use this historical data

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:37.288
to give us an idea of
the impact of temperature

00:40:37.288 --> 00:40:38.121
and mortality.

00:40:38.121 --> 00:40:40.140
I should say this is really variation

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:41.640
around business as usual.

00:40:41.640 --> 00:40:44.490
It doesn't include any of the
disaster that we're gonna get

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:45.933
to as well.

00:40:46.837 --> 00:40:49.803
Flooding and this type, you
know, fire this type of stuff.

00:40:49.803 --> 00:40:51.930
It's just temperature.

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.090
So this is very much lower bound,

00:40:54.090 --> 00:40:57.150
but we are gonna stick
with that for the moment.

00:40:57.150 --> 00:41:01.650
And so what they're
doing in their QJE paper

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:05.760
is to look at a bunch of micro regions,

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:06.810
they're fewer than that,

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:09.813
there's a bit of interpolation
involved in that.

00:41:09.813 --> 00:41:12.630
And then separating
countries by cold countries,

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:15.180
hot countries, medium temperate countries

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:19.110
and then poor country, rich
country, medium countries.

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:24.110
And two things. First of all,
in places that are very cold,

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:27.270
when it gets hot suddenly
a lot of people die

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.365
'cause the places are not used to that.

00:41:30.365 --> 00:41:32.010
So a few now,

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:35.460
almost a decade ago there was
a massive hit wave in France

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:37.290
and many, many people died

00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:39.270
'cause we were so unprepared.

00:41:39.270 --> 00:41:42.367
In the subsequent hit
wave we did much better

00:41:42.367 --> 00:41:43.590
because we kind of knew
what to do with that

00:41:43.590 --> 00:41:47.190
and visit the old people
and bring them to cinemas

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:49.640
or museum or so on.

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:50.820
So that's the first thing.

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:54.513
The second thing is that
in places which are poor,

00:41:55.680 --> 00:41:58.620
mortality is much higher for hot days.

00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:02.010
And this is because you don't
have you poor people tend

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:06.819
to work outside, poor people
do not have air conditioning.

00:42:06.819 --> 00:42:08.520
So you take a very hot day in Texas

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:10.320
and people go from the
air conditioning home

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:11.790
into the air conditioning car,

00:42:11.790 --> 00:42:14.400
into the air conditioning
office and they don't die.

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:16.530
But you're very, very hot day in Pakistan

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:20.250
and then people are working in a heat

00:42:20.250 --> 00:42:22.920
and they have heat stroke and they die.

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:25.083
So when you combine all of that,

00:42:25.083 --> 00:42:26.040
so this is what graph summarize,

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:27.060
when you combine all of that,

00:42:27.060 --> 00:42:29.580
you get this maps, which
is the mortality effect

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:31.263
of a particular hot day.

00:42:32.210 --> 00:42:33.750
You know, one more hot day, 35 degrees.

00:42:33.750 --> 00:42:35.522
So of course in, you know,

00:42:35.522 --> 00:42:38.220
in Greenland it would be very people,

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:39.960
if there were people in Greenland,

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:41.760
they would die when it's very hot.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:43.860
But there are not going
to be very many hot days

00:42:43.860 --> 00:42:45.270
in Greenland.

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:47.850
But then in Sub-Saharan
Africa where we already see

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:49.380
that there would be a lot of hot days,

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:53.313
then a lot of people will die
too because they are poor.

00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:58.863
So when you combine the fact that, oops!

00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:03.915
I'm not doing very well with
the slide, yes, that's the one,

00:43:03.915 --> 00:43:06.090
one when you combine the two,

00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:10.339
you the fact that there will
be more hot days in poor places

00:43:10.339 --> 00:43:12.600
that are already poor and
these hot days will create

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:14.820
more death.

00:43:14.820 --> 00:43:18.690
You get this map also
from that same QGE paper,

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:21.240
which is by 2100.

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:23.610
So maybe it's too find the
future for us to think,

00:43:23.610 --> 00:43:25.131
'cause I think

00:43:25.131 --> 00:43:27.000
we probably could do something
already like that today.

00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:28.770
But it's very striking,

00:43:28.770 --> 00:43:32.500
which is you have this big red blood blot

00:43:33.543 --> 00:43:37.880
in basically Kelsey's field work,

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:41.160
the whole, the South region of Africa,

00:43:41.160 --> 00:43:45.540
but also the North of South Asia.

00:43:45.540 --> 00:43:48.660
And then most of the OECD's blue,

