01:12:49.529 --> 01:12:51.180
and basically I have little to say,

01:12:51.180 --> 01:12:53.372
I think we have, you know, 15

01:12:53.372 --> 01:12:55.470
or 20 minutes of questions,
so I'd like to just engage

01:12:55.470 --> 01:12:58.590
with the audience and see what you'd like

01:12:58.590 --> 01:13:00.903
to ask about these great presentations.

01:13:05.490 --> 01:13:06.323
Yeah, Ken,

01:13:09.000 --> 01:13:10.511
- Great.

01:13:10.511 --> 01:13:11.970
Thank you very much for the
very impressive presentations

01:13:11.970 --> 01:13:14.610
and I appreciate the posing of a solution

01:13:14.610 --> 01:13:18.750
after a very sobering
assessment of where we stand.

01:13:18.750 --> 01:13:23.750
My question relates to the,
this is for Professor Duflo

01:13:24.210 --> 01:13:27.600
on the 500 billion that you posed.

01:13:27.600 --> 01:13:30.690
So that was coming from
the mortality costs

01:13:30.690 --> 01:13:34.080
that are being imposed
upon developing countries,

01:13:34.080 --> 01:13:35.790
but that 500 billion also depends

01:13:35.790 --> 01:13:37.710
on the value of statistical life.

01:13:37.710 --> 01:13:39.030
That's assumed.

01:13:39.030 --> 01:13:43.082
And there are contentious
arguments all the time

01:13:43.082 --> 01:13:46.290
about do we count someone
in Ghana the same way

01:13:46.290 --> 01:13:48.193
as someone in the US

01:13:48.193 --> 01:13:51.390
or much less depending on, you
know, scale it by their GDP.

01:13:51.390 --> 01:13:54.300
So I'm wondering whether
that opens a can of worms

01:13:54.300 --> 01:13:59.300
by tying it to the 500
billion to something

01:13:59.310 --> 01:14:01.380
that is attached to a
value of statistical life

01:14:01.380 --> 01:14:03.390
that may be very
contentious among the people

01:14:03.390 --> 01:14:04.920
who would be receiving the funding.

01:14:04.920 --> 01:14:06.203
Do you have thoughts on this?

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:09.510
- Yeah, great question. So there's that.

01:14:09.510 --> 01:14:10.710
And then on the other hand you could say,

01:14:10.710 --> 01:14:12.810
but we know why stop at mortality,

01:14:12.810 --> 01:14:15.600
we should use the full
social cost of carbon

01:14:15.600 --> 01:14:17.670
or at least the social cost of carbon

01:14:17.670 --> 01:14:20.013
as it plays out in the poor countries.

01:14:23.151 --> 01:14:27.750
And I think that's maybe
would be Tamara's view

01:14:27.750 --> 01:14:29.640
or Michael s view,

01:14:29.640 --> 01:14:30.473
but I,

01:14:31.620 --> 01:14:35.338
I think it might be the
most helpful way to do this.

01:14:35.338 --> 01:14:36.831
And that's kind of why I,

01:14:36.831 --> 01:14:39.614
where I hope to go is to
choose your own adventure.

01:14:39.614 --> 01:14:42.330
You know, this is the emissions

01:14:42.330 --> 01:14:44.520
you can even go production, consumption.

01:14:44.520 --> 01:14:45.630
The new,

01:14:45.630 --> 01:14:46.463
the new

01:14:46.463 --> 01:14:50.160
and improved consumption that
they're going to generate.

01:14:50.160 --> 01:14:51.540
And then this is the cost.

01:14:51.540 --> 01:14:56.540
This is, you know, $37 for
value of a statistical life

01:14:56.925 --> 01:15:00.870
times the mortality damage
full social cost of carbon

01:15:00.870 --> 01:15:04.710
and their value people
in low income country,

01:15:04.710 --> 01:15:06.787
which I wouldn't personally do,

01:15:06.787 --> 01:15:08.424
but you know, someone might want to do.

01:15:08.424 --> 01:15:10.830
And that gives you a sense of where,

01:15:10.830 --> 01:15:15.600
how this numbers might
play out for the one thing

01:15:15.600 --> 01:15:18.840
that I hope to do is
kind of build this tool.

01:15:18.840 --> 01:15:21.870
All of the data is already there to do it

01:15:21.870 --> 01:15:25.620
and it would help people form their,

01:15:25.620 --> 01:15:28.800
but at least they
would've to argue publicly

01:15:28.800 --> 01:15:30.480
that that's what they choose.

01:15:30.480 --> 01:15:32.930
And that would, that itself
would be interesting.

01:15:34.680 --> 01:15:35.643
- Yes, Catherine,

01:15:37.890 --> 01:15:40.190
- [Catherine] Fascinating
proposal. Thank you.

01:15:41.157 --> 01:15:42.750
I guess I'm more optimistic

01:15:42.750 --> 01:15:44.700
than you are about carbon pricing.

01:15:44.700 --> 01:15:48.840
I think in particular the
EU CBAM is encouraging a lot

01:15:48.840 --> 01:15:51.210
of countries, like just
yesterday Turkey announced

01:15:51.210 --> 01:15:54.330
that they're adopting carbon pricing.

01:15:54.330 --> 01:15:57.330
There are a bunch of countries,
Thailand, Vietnam, Senegal,

01:15:57.330 --> 01:16:01.050
that are considering
carbon pricing in response

01:16:01.050 --> 01:16:02.790
to the EU CBAM.

01:16:02.790 --> 01:16:05.400
And there have been bills in the US

01:16:05.400 --> 01:16:10.400
that tie carbon price
revenues to distributions

01:16:11.310 --> 01:16:13.335
to low income countries.

01:16:13.335 --> 01:16:14.910
And I could imagine that that would make

01:16:14.910 --> 01:16:17.850
it more politically,

01:16:17.850 --> 01:16:21.270
you know, saleable in
developing countries,

01:16:21.270 --> 01:16:26.160
or sorry, richer countries
that people might be, you know,

01:16:26.160 --> 01:16:28.320
I think first of all, like
communicating the fact

01:16:28.320 --> 01:16:30.030
that carbon pricing raise revenue

01:16:30.030 --> 01:16:33.450
and then using that revenue
for low income countries

01:16:33.450 --> 01:16:38.370
could be something that makes
it more politically tenable

01:16:38.370 --> 01:16:39.693
in rich countries.

01:16:41.898 --> 01:16:44.940
- Yeah, I don't think that
carbon pricing cannot be done.

01:16:44.940 --> 01:16:46.590
I think that they can,

01:16:46.590 --> 01:16:48.990
but I'm not sure that if
you link carbon pricing

01:16:48.990 --> 01:16:50.460
to sending the money in poor countries,

01:16:50.460 --> 01:16:52.980
that would work out in
a country like in France

01:16:52.980 --> 01:16:54.570
for example, that wouldn't play out.

01:16:54.570 --> 01:16:55.713
It's like whatever.

01:16:56.760 --> 01:16:59.670
Because people are so,
you know, the poor people,

01:16:59.670 --> 01:17:01.590
they feel so much hardship

01:17:01.590 --> 01:17:04.470
as it is even though their
hardship is not the same

01:17:04.470 --> 01:17:09.210
as people in Niger that,
you know, they don't want,

01:17:09.210 --> 01:17:11.490
they are not concerned
about future generation.

01:17:11.490 --> 01:17:14.070
They're not particularly
concerned about people in Africa

01:17:14.070 --> 01:17:15.720
either if they have to pay for it.

01:17:15.720 --> 01:17:18.420
So, so I think urban pricing,

01:17:18.420 --> 01:17:20.940
that's another discussion in
a way I think urban pricing

01:17:20.940 --> 01:17:23.340
can work if you can credibly commit

01:17:23.340 --> 01:17:25.410
that the incidence is not on the poor.

01:17:25.410 --> 01:17:27.153
And I think it has been difficult,

01:17:29.220 --> 01:17:31.230
but it has worked in
some cases, for example,

01:17:31.230 --> 01:17:35.160
Indonesia successfully
removed a lot of subsidies

01:17:35.160 --> 01:17:36.780
to it's not carbon pricing,

01:17:36.780 --> 01:17:39.630
but it's equivalent when they tied

01:17:39.630 --> 01:17:41.760
it to an unconditional cash transfer.

01:17:41.760 --> 01:17:43.440
And I think there was enough trust

01:17:43.440 --> 01:17:45.150
that it just went through.

01:17:45.150 --> 01:17:47.520
Canada was able to do that as well in the,

01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:49.440
at the same time when France tried

01:17:49.440 --> 01:17:51.420
to do car imposes carbon tax,

01:17:51.420 --> 01:17:54.270
like just before they had
removed the Wealth Tax.

01:17:54.270 --> 01:17:58.950
So the optic of it was, it's
kind of a different discussion.

01:17:58.950 --> 01:18:01.080
I think carbon pricing are feasible,

01:18:01.080 --> 01:18:05.040
but they require a lot
of trust and pedagogy

01:18:05.040 --> 01:18:08.970
and I'm not sure that linking
them to poor countries

01:18:08.970 --> 01:18:11.040
would make it easier. But maybe.

01:18:11.040 --> 01:18:13.200
- [Catherine] It just,
I mean you could read,

01:18:13.200 --> 01:18:15.360
so what what's been proposed
in bills in Congress

01:18:15.360 --> 01:18:18.843
is to like link some of it like,
you know, 25% or something.

01:18:20.430 --> 01:18:22.200
- Maybe to jump in here.

01:18:22.200 --> 01:18:27.200
So I fully agree with Esther
that national carbon taxes

01:18:27.400 --> 01:18:32.400
would probably fail in so many
countries if they were tied,

01:18:32.460 --> 01:18:37.440
if their revenues were tied
with increased spending

01:18:37.440 --> 01:18:39.480
or donations to the rest of the world.

01:18:39.480 --> 01:18:43.050
But in the CBAMs, so border adjustments,

01:18:43.050 --> 01:18:45.450
there were proposals actually in the EU

01:18:45.450 --> 01:18:48.540
that some of the revenues
of the border would be used

01:18:48.540 --> 01:18:49.650
to compensate the losers

01:18:49.650 --> 01:18:50.820
because in that case the losers

01:18:50.820 --> 01:18:52.560
are outside basically, right?

01:18:52.560 --> 01:18:54.870
But that battle so far was lost.

01:18:54.870 --> 01:18:59.870
So the EU decided to keep
the funds for within.

01:19:00.060 --> 01:19:02.370
But I think this is going
to be an ongoing debate

01:19:02.370 --> 01:19:04.350
as basically the rates

01:19:04.350 --> 01:19:07.620
or the implied rates
increase at the border

01:19:07.620 --> 01:19:10.140
or the types of goods that are subject

01:19:10.140 --> 01:19:12.960
to this mechanism get enlarged.

01:19:12.960 --> 01:19:16.260
So I think the same principle
that applies within countries

01:19:16.260 --> 01:19:18.270
that carbon pricing works

01:19:18.270 --> 01:19:20.730
when the losers are well compensated,

01:19:20.730 --> 01:19:23.280
CF British Columbia for instance,

01:19:23.280 --> 01:19:26.820
I think it also applies pretty
well in the global stage.

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:29.650
So I think there's still
scope for these discussions

01:19:30.590 --> 01:19:32.130
to be pursued in Europe

01:19:32.130 --> 01:19:35.433
and in other regions
and countries as well.

01:19:38.839 --> 01:19:39.672
- Yes.

01:19:43.620 --> 01:19:46.260
- Thank you. Very
impressive presentations.

01:19:46.260 --> 01:19:47.850
Thank you much indeed.

01:19:47.850 --> 01:19:48.840
My name is Chaba,

01:19:48.840 --> 01:19:52.533
I'm coming from the field
of diplomatic struggles.

01:19:54.122 --> 01:19:55.463
You have indicated a couple of times,

01:19:56.330 --> 01:19:59.910
a couple of questions that
may help us moving forward.

01:19:59.910 --> 01:20:00.743
First,

01:20:03.960 --> 01:20:05.970
I understand that we basically,

01:20:05.970 --> 01:20:09.090
we are talking about a global system

01:20:09.090 --> 01:20:13.953
of redistributing revenues and incomes.

01:20:14.880 --> 01:20:16.770
On national scales,

01:20:16.770 --> 01:20:19.590
we have the legal and
institution system for that.

01:20:19.590 --> 01:20:23.730
We call it Taxation and Social Policies.

01:20:23.730 --> 01:20:26.790
Do we have it on a global scale

01:20:26.790 --> 01:20:30.840
and are we going to
introduce it at global scale?

01:20:30.840 --> 01:20:31.863
The second,

01:20:33.660 --> 01:20:35.880
you have mentioned that in the range

01:20:35.880 --> 01:20:38.560
of 500, 3,500, $60 billion

01:20:40.583 --> 01:20:43.773
might be raised, might be
needed to make a change,

01:20:44.640 --> 01:20:48.900
which is about three times of the size

01:20:48.900 --> 01:20:50.313
of the official ODA.

01:20:52.140 --> 01:20:57.140
Can you tell me one single
country in the last 70 years

01:20:57.960 --> 01:21:01.120
that came out of poverty due to ODA

01:21:02.640 --> 01:21:07.640
and if the tripling size
would make a difference,

01:21:10.290 --> 01:21:12.933
how that would make the difference?

01:21:14.417 --> 01:21:16.710
And the third one,

01:21:16.710 --> 01:21:20.890
I was very much interested in
where do you see the sources

01:21:21.826 --> 01:21:24.426
of these funds that would
be globally redistributed?

01:21:25.391 --> 01:21:29.370
And you never mentioned
the petrol countries,

01:21:29.370 --> 01:21:31.391
just in the last one

01:21:31.391 --> 01:21:33.990
and a half years, the Windfall profit

01:21:33.990 --> 01:21:38.751
was much higher than
anything else in the last

01:21:38.751 --> 01:21:42.240
1,000 years in the human
history, just Norway,

01:21:42.240 --> 01:21:45.370
during one and a half year received

01:21:46.503 --> 01:21:48.630
extra windfall profit without investment,

01:21:48.630 --> 01:21:50.490
without an inspection investment.

01:21:50.490 --> 01:21:54.480
That would be in real
terms, real prices today,

01:21:54.480 --> 01:21:58.380
much higher than the whole
Marshall plan offered

01:21:58.380 --> 01:22:01.170
by the US to the whole of Europe.

01:22:01.170 --> 01:22:04.563
And that is now is out of our thinking.

01:22:05.610 --> 01:22:06.443
Thank you.

01:22:09.219 --> 01:22:10.513
- Yes, sure.

01:22:10.513 --> 01:22:13.174
So we need a couple minutes left,

01:22:13.174 --> 01:22:14.670
so I'm just going to
collect two, three questions

01:22:14.670 --> 01:22:18.330
and then allow the panelists to respond.

01:22:18.330 --> 01:22:19.920
Yeah, so cost us.

01:22:19.920 --> 01:22:22.248
- Yeah, thanks for the present.

01:22:22.248 --> 01:22:23.423
It was super illuminating,

01:22:23.423 --> 01:22:25.987
the numbers for inequality in particular,

01:22:25.987 --> 01:22:27.330
the numbers for inequality,

01:22:27.330 --> 01:22:29.703
I find the numbers for getting out of,

01:22:30.919 --> 01:22:32.100
you know, the carbon
cost of getting people,

01:22:32.100 --> 01:22:34.930
getting out of poverty, kind of optimistic

01:22:36.006 --> 01:22:37.950
because if we think of the
countries that, you know,

01:22:37.950 --> 01:22:41.640
grew fast out poverty like
India, China, Vietnam,

01:22:41.640 --> 01:22:44.853
they went through a growth
process that was much more messy

01:22:44.853 --> 01:22:47.490
and the emissions increased massively,

01:22:47.490 --> 01:22:50.327
also for general
equilibrium regions, right?

01:22:50.327 --> 01:22:52.680
Like, you know, you have
to general create policies

01:22:52.680 --> 01:22:56.133
that have many repercussions
of how you grow, how you emit.

01:22:56.970 --> 01:23:00.300
But if this is a true
critique what I'm saying,

01:23:00.300 --> 01:23:03.000
then this has implication
of how do you, you know,

01:23:03.000 --> 01:23:04.975
what policies should you use?

01:23:04.975 --> 01:23:06.780
'Cause redistribution
is not gonna be enough.

01:23:06.780 --> 01:23:09.280
So I just wanted to hear
your thoughts about that.

01:23:11.261 --> 01:23:13.293
- And I guess lastly,

01:23:14.760 --> 01:23:15.593
yeah,

01:23:15.593 --> 01:23:16.503
so Brian, you want,

01:23:17.530 --> 01:23:18.510
- I had two rules.

01:23:18.510 --> 01:23:20.040
One, I had one question on my own

01:23:20.040 --> 01:23:24.273
and then there was one on
Zoom from actually a panelist

01:23:24.273 --> 01:23:25.106
who was in the afternoon

01:23:25.106 --> 01:23:27.242
and she sent it from India
where she stayed up late.

01:23:27.242 --> 01:23:28.075
So I felt I should ask that.

01:23:28.075 --> 01:23:30.660
I think my question,
which was a quick question

01:23:30.660 --> 01:23:33.180
is that this poverty emissions trade off,

01:23:33.180 --> 01:23:35.430
which I'm incredibly
sympathetic to politically

01:23:35.430 --> 01:23:38.130
otherwise, my understanding
is it doesn't account

01:23:38.130 --> 01:23:41.790
for changes in growth trajectories
of these poor countries.

01:23:41.790 --> 01:23:42.810
And so you can imagine

01:23:42.810 --> 01:23:45.720
that if Niger suddenly
starts growing at 20%

01:23:45.720 --> 01:23:48.510
or something like that based
on fossil fuel development,

01:23:48.510 --> 01:23:51.240
you may see very, you may
actually see a genuine trade off.

01:23:51.240 --> 01:23:53.610
And I think that's the
thing that people are posing

01:23:53.610 --> 01:23:55.560
is not so much that if you redistribute

01:23:56.583 --> 01:23:57.416
and open them up, how
much they'll consume,

01:23:57.416 --> 01:23:59.820
but rather if that country really changes

01:23:59.820 --> 01:24:01.796
sort of this nature of growth.

01:24:01.796 --> 01:24:02.629
So I'd love to hear about that.

01:24:02.629 --> 01:24:07.050
And then this is from
Tarji who's in India.

01:24:07.050 --> 01:24:10.170
She's one of the IPCC
authors in the afternoon,

01:24:10.170 --> 01:24:11.003
for Lucas,

01:24:11.003 --> 01:24:13.530
does the focus on income
distributions across the world

01:24:13.530 --> 01:24:15.540
as though we have one
global community divided

01:24:15.540 --> 01:24:17.370
into three classes, not
take away from the fact

01:24:17.370 --> 01:24:19.200
that these trends are
still strongly mediated

01:24:19.200 --> 01:24:20.820
by nation states.

01:24:20.820 --> 01:24:23.130
How do we connect this inequality
across different levels

01:24:23.130 --> 01:24:25.380
of development to nation states?

01:24:25.380 --> 01:24:28.170
And then to Esther,

01:24:28.170 --> 01:24:30.810
why only focus on the
eradication of basic minimum

01:24:30.810 --> 01:24:31.680
or extreme poverty?

01:24:31.680 --> 01:24:33.060
The study quoted is often used

01:24:33.060 --> 01:24:35.583
to shut down arguments of climate justice.

01:24:37.645 --> 01:24:39.420
- So maybe I could ask
Lucas to respond first

01:24:39.420 --> 01:24:42.633
and then Esther to take the
several questions at once.

01:24:44.680 --> 01:24:45.513
- Many thanks.

01:24:45.513 --> 01:24:46.560
Maybe on the first point

01:24:46.560 --> 01:24:51.090
on the carbon impact
of poverty eradication.

01:24:51.090 --> 01:24:53.740
And so I think this is a valid point

01:24:54.900 --> 01:24:59.610
and co-authors have a paper
on that basically saying that,

01:24:59.610 --> 01:25:01.020
you know, after Bruckner

01:25:01.020 --> 01:25:05.010
that actually it might be a
little more carbon intensive.

01:25:05.010 --> 01:25:09.480
Although to me the real
challenge is what happens

01:25:09.480 --> 01:25:12.940
